Image: One of the darkest chapters in the California Gold Rush, in American history, and in America’s relations with indigenous peoples: 

The California Genocide.  

Between 1846 and 1870, California’s Native American population was reduced from 150,000 to 30,000, an 80 percent decline.  And while most of this has been attributed to disease and to starvation, a substantial number (between 9,000 and 16,000) have been attributed to violent deaths. 

The State of California organized volunteer militias that were directly charged with hunting and killing Native Americans.  

The U.S. Federal Government not only reimbursed the State of California for these expenses, it also authorized the U.S. military to engage in these same practices.  

Other California municipalities also offered their own bounties for Native Americans. 

The killings were part of a wider - and systematic - effort to disenfranchise and even enslave Native Americans in California, through a form of indentured servitude, authorized by Law in 1850 (“Act for the Government and Protection of Indians”). 

Framed - and justified - as “protection,” it was institutionalized murder. 

In 2019, California Gov. Gavin Newsom issued an official apology where he explicitly used the term “genocide.”  In a separate executive order, he also established a “Truth and Healing Council” to promote deeper understanding of this event and to prepare a comprehensive report that will include public policy recommendations, including reparations. 

The report is scheduled to be released in the Fall of this year.  

